+### Construction and hierarchy
+
+Sequential and explicitly indexed assembly are equivalent for the same
+completed ordered records. The first successful add fixes the mode. Indexed
+assembly accepts nonnegative positions in any order and requires a contiguous
+zero-based path at build. A valid add to an occupied index replaces that input
+without growing the path. Validate additions before changing any assembly state.
+At assembly only, a null or empty qualifier becomes `N<position>` using the final
+zero-based position. The corresponding explicit qualifier has identical
+identity, matching, ordering and interchange semantics. A null or empty entry
+version means absence. Failed adds and builds preserve builder state and
+previous snapshots. Builders remain reusable. Completed Topics and their
+entries are immutable.
+
+Literal ancestry compares whole qualifier/value entries in order. A strict
+ancestor is a proper prefix, independently of versions. Depth counts entries.
+A root has depth one and no parent. Parent and ancestor projections preserve
+versions for retained entries. Valid ancestor depths range from one through
+the full depth. Reject invalid depths. A null candidate has no ancestry.
+Natural order compares qualifier and then value at each
+position, using case-sensitive locale-independent lexical order and putting
+the shorter path first when it is a proper prefix. Comparison returns zero
+exactly for equal Topics.
+
+### Representation and interchange
+
+The canonical name joins qualified entries `qualifier=value` with unescaped
+periods. The versioned canonical name uses `qualifier:version=value` when a
+version is present and omits the version segment when absent. Both forms
+escape literal periods, equals signs, colons and backslashes in fields with a
+backslash. Canonical parsers require valid explicit qualifiers and values.
+They reject malformed separators and escapes and explicit empty version
+segments. The unversioned parser rejects version-bearing syntax. Parsing
+reproduces the identity projection or all ordered field data, respectively.
+
+The unqualified name joins raw values with periods. The friendly name is the
+raw leaf value. The display name is the leaf value at depth one and the last
+two raw values joined by a hyphen at greater depths. The relative name is the
+escaped last qualified entry without version. The unqualified, friendly and
+display projections are for their defined non-identity uses and can be
+ambiguous. Logical interchange preserves ordered completed entries and
+optional versions, reconstructs validated immutable values, and preserves that
+order independently of the encoding. Assembly indices supply ordering and
+have no independent identity or mandatory interchange field. Field validation
+and meaning are independent of the selected serializer.
